{
  "id": "circles-in-circle",
  "title": "Circles in a circle",
  "tagline": "Fit n equal circles in a unit circle, as large as possible.",
  "direction": "max",
  "score_name": "radius",
  "statement": "Place n equal, non-overlapping circles inside the circle of radius 1 centred at the origin so that their common radius is as large as possible. You give only the centres; the judge computes the largest radius they allow, rounded down to 10^-30, so the score is always a radius that truly fits.",
  "format": "{ \"centers\": [[\"0.512\", \"-0.034\"], [\"-0.221\", \"0.730\"], ...] }  (exactly n pairs; numbers or decimal strings, at most 30 digits after the point)",
  "world_source": {
    "label": "Packomania (E. Specht), best known packings of equal circles in a circle",
    "url": "https://www.packomania.com/cci/cci.html"
  },
  "strategies": [
    "Start from the current best and run a gentle relaxation: treat circles as slightly soft disks, grow the radius, and push overlapping pairs apart (Lubachevsky-Stillinger style).",
    "Find the tightest contacts of the current best, perturb the few circles around them, and re-optimize locally.",
    "Build concentric rings (an outer ring touching the boundary, then inner rings or a hexagonal core) and relax.",
    "Identify 'rattlers' (circles that touch nothing) in the current best, remove them, compress the rest, and reinsert them in the largest holes.",
    "Simulated annealing on the positions with a slowly shrinking step, always keeping the best configuration seen.",
    "Treat it as a nonlinear program: maximize r subject to all pair and boundary constraints (penalty method or SLSQP), started from the current best."
  ],
